---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-in-brazil`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D in Brazil',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Vitamin D deficiency', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E55', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D in Brazil', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D in Brazil: A Sunny Country With Real Deficiency in the South"
  description="Brazil straddles the equator, yet deficiency is common in its southern, higher-latitude cities and among older and darker-skinned people. The latitude gradient and what it means."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-in-brazil", label: "Vitamin D in Brazil" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D in Brazil</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Brazil is enormous, spanning from about 5°N in the Amazon to 33°S at the Uruguayan border — so
      talking about "vitamin D in Brazil" as one thing is misleading. The tropical north is bathed in
      year-round UVB; the subtropical south, around Porto Alegre and Curitiba, has genuine winters and
      a measurable vitamin D dip. The country contains both extremes of the story this site tells.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The internal latitude gradient</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Brazilian studies show exactly the pattern latitude predicts: deficiency is uncommon near the
      equator and rises as you move south. Winter 25(OH)D levels in southern cities can fall into the
      insufficient range for a substantial share of the population, mirroring what happens at similar
      latitudes elsewhere. It is a natural experiment in how much geography, rather than nationality,
      drives vitamin D status. See <a href="/vitamin-d-by-latitude" class="text-amber-700 underline">vitamin D by latitude</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why a sunny country still has deficiency</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Even in sunny regions, Brazilian surveys — especially in older adults and postmenopausal women —
      find surprisingly high rates of low vitamin D. The reasons are familiar: urban indoor lifestyles,
      sun avoidance and sunscreen use (Brazil has an active skin-cancer prevention culture), and darker
      skin across much of the population, which requires more UVB for the same synthesis. Institutional
      and housebound elderly people are the most affected group of all.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Guidance and testing</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Brazilian endocrinology and pathology societies have issued guidance emphasising that not everyone
      needs testing — routine screening of healthy young adults is discouraged — but that testing and
      treatment are warranted in at-risk groups: the elderly, pregnant women, people with osteoporosis,
      malabsorption, or chronic kidney disease. Target and dosing broadly follow international norms,
      with sufficiency generally set at 20–30 ng/mL depending on the group. Fortification is limited, so
      diet contributes modestly.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical takeaway</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>In the tropical north, most healthy, active people make ample vitamin D year-round.</li>
      <li>In the subtropical south, expect a winter dip — at-risk people should consider supplements.</li>
      <li>Older adults, darker-skinned individuals, and the housebound are the priority everywhere in Brazil.</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Living in a sunny country is not a guarantee of adequate
      vitamin D. If you are in an at-risk group, ask your doctor about testing rather than assuming the
      climate has you covered.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-in-brazil" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
